      <div class="prose prose-lg prose-blue max-w-none text-gray-700 leading-relaxed">
        <p class="text-2xl font-medium text-gray-900 mb-8 italic">
          If you only play recreational pickleball, nobody is going to check your paddle. If you play a sanctioned tournament, your paddle needs to appear as a pass on the USA Pickleball approved paddle list, and it is your responsibility to make sure it does. Approval is not a quality rating. It means a paddle model was submitted to USA Pickleball and passed tests on its dimensions, its surface and how much power and spin it can put on the ball. Those standards have tightened in the last two years, a handful of paddles have been removed from the list, and a new spin test took effect on October 1, 2026. Here is what that means for the paddle you own or are about to buy.
        </p>

        <TopPick />

        <div id="what-approval" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            1. What USA Pickleball Approval Actually Means
          </h2>

          <p>
            USA Pickleball is the national governing body for the sport in the United States. It publishes the official rulebook used in sanctioned amateur play, and it runs an equipment program that certifies paddles and balls against that rulebook. A paddle that passes is added to the approved paddle list and the manufacturer can print the USA Pickleball Approved mark on it. A paddle that has not been submitted, or has not passed, does not appear as a pass and should not carry the mark.
          </p>

          <p>
            Approval is a compliance check, not a recommendation. It tells you a paddle model met the equipment standards when it was tested. It says nothing about whether the paddle is comfortable, durable or a good match for your game. Plenty of excellent paddles are approved, and so are plenty of mediocre ones. Our <a href="/best-pickleball-paddle/">best pickleball paddles</a> roundup covers how they actually play; this guide covers whether you can use them in a sanctioned event.
          </p>

          <p>
            Approval is also tied to a specific model, not to a brand. A manufacturer can have some paddles approved and others not, and a structural change such as a different thickness, shape or core counts as a different paddle that needs its own certification. Cosmetic changes like a new color are usually handled with lighter paperwork. When you look a paddle up, match the exact model name and thickness rather than assuming a whole line is covered.
          </p>

          <p>
            Finally, USA Pickleball is not the only body that maintains a list. Professional tours have run their own equipment programs at various times, and a paddle can be legal in USA Pickleball sanctioned amateur events without being legal on a particular pro tour, or the reverse. For most amateur players the USA Pickleball list is the one that matters, but if you play an event run under another organization's rules, check which list it uses.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="what-tested" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            2. What the Approval Tests Cover
          </h2>

          <p>
            The details live in USA Pickleball's equipment standards documentation, which is revised periodically. In broad terms, a paddle submitted for certification is checked against several categories, and it has to pass all of them. The specific thresholds for some tests are set in that documentation rather than in the rulebook itself, so the summary below sticks to what each category covers.
          </p>

          <div class="overflow-hidden border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl my-8">
            <div class="overflow-x-auto">
              <table class="w-full text-left">
                <thead class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-200">
                  <tr>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Test area</th>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">What it checks</th>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Source of the limit</th>
                  </tr>
                </thead>
                <tbody>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Size</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Combined length and width no more than 24 in, length no more than 17 in, including edge guard and butt cap</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Rulebook (2025 edition, Section 2.E.3)</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Surface</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Face texture, historically measured as surface roughness, and friction</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Equipment standards</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Construction</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Rules on materials and features, such as no moving parts or springs that add energy</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Rulebook and equipment standards</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Power (PBCoR)</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">How fast the ball rebounds relative to impact speed</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Equipment standards, limit tightened in 2024 and 2025</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Spin rate</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Measured spin on a standardized shot, for paddles certified from Oct 1, 2026</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Equipment standards, announced July 2026</td>
                  </tr>
                </tbody>
              </table>
            </div>
          </div>

          <p>
            <strong>Size</strong> is the simplest and the least likely to trip up a modern paddle. The rulebook caps combined length and width at 24 inches and length at 17 inches, and every major brand builds inside that. There is no thickness limit and no weight limit in the amateur rulebook, which is why you see 16mm and even thicker paddles and a wide spread of weights. Our comparison of <a href="/elongated-vs-standard-pickleball-paddle/">elongated and standard paddles</a> explains how brands spend that size allowance.
          </p>

          <p>
            <strong>Surface</strong> rules exist because a rougher face grips the ball and adds spin. For years the main control was a limit on surface roughness, measured on the face of the paddle. Grit, textured coatings and raw carbon faces are all designed to increase friction, and paddles have been delisted after production samples measured rougher than the approved sample. This is the area most affected by the new spin test, covered below.
          </p>

          <p>
            <strong>Construction</strong> rules cover things like a ban on moving parts and features that store and release energy. These rarely come up with mainstream paddles, but they are the reason novelty designs sometimes struggle to get approved. Then there is the <strong>power</strong> test, PBCoR, which has become the most consequential test of the last two years and gets its own section.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="pbcor" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            3. The PBCoR Power Limit, Explained
          </h2>

          <p>
            PBCoR stands for Paddle/Ball Coefficient of Restitution. It measures how fast a ball comes off the paddle relative to how fast it hit it. A paddle that returns more of the ball's speed has a higher PBCoR and plays with more pop. USA Pickleball announced the test in August 2024, saying it had been developed over several months with industry partners, and introduced it in the last quarter of 2024 as a formal power standard for certification.
          </p>

          <p>
            The limit has tightened since then. According to manufacturer compliance pages and industry reporting, the not-to-exceed PBCoR value was 0.44 from November 1, 2024, and was lowered to 0.43 from November 1, 2025. Some sources also report that a paddle is expected to stay within the limit across its useful life, which matters because some cores get livelier as they break in. Check USA Pickleball's current equipment standards for the authoritative figure, since these numbers can change.
          </p>

          <p>
            The reason for the test was a wave of very powerful paddles, many using thermoformed construction and foam-filled or reinforced cores. Some of these paddles reportedly gained power with use as their cores broke down, which raised both competitive and safety concerns. Our explainer on <a href="/thermoformed-pickleball-paddle/">thermoformed paddles</a> covers why that construction tends to be powerful and why it drew so much attention.
          </p>

          <p>
            For a buyer, PBCoR has two practical consequences. First, an approved paddle bought today is limited in how much raw pop it can deliver, so marketing that promises dramatically more power than other approved paddles should be read with caution. Second, a paddle that was approved under an older limit, or one whose production samples later tested hotter than the original, can be removed from the list. That is exactly what has happened to several well-known paddles.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="spin-test" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            4. The New Spin Test From October 2026
          </h2>

          <p>
            Surface roughness was always an indirect way of controlling spin. A rough face tends to produce more spin, but the relationship is not perfect, and manufacturers found ways to increase spin without exceeding roughness limits. In July 2026 USA Pickleball announced a direct test: a spin rate test that measures the actual RPM of a ball struck in a standardized way, using a dual-camera measurement system.
          </p>

          <p>
            According to reporting on the announcement, paddles certified after October 1, 2026 must pass the spin test with a value under 2,100 RPM. Paddles certified before that date are held to the standards in effect when they were certified, so the change is not retroactive for paddles already on the list. During the transition, paddles that passed the existing surface tests could still be added to the list before October 1. USA Pickleball has said it will continue to measure surface roughness and friction; how those measurements are used alongside the spin limit is something to confirm in its current standards.
          </p>

          <p>
            If you are buying a new paddle now, the practical effect is modest. Any newly certified paddle has been held to the spin limit, and older approved paddles remain legal under the rules they were approved under. If spin is a priority, choose based on how a paddle actually plays rather than on spin claims alone. Our <a href="/best-pickleball-paddle-for-spin/">best paddles for spin</a> guide looks at the surface and construction choices that matter within the rules.
          </p>

          <div class="bg-gray-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-6 lg:p-8 my-8">
            <p class="text-xs font-black uppercase tracking-widest text-[#38BDF8] mb-3">WHAT CHANGED</p>
            <p class="text-xl font-bold text-white mb-3">From October 1, 2026, newly certified paddles must stay under a 2,100 RPM spin limit.</p>
            <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed m-0">The test was announced in July 2026 and measures spin directly with cameras. Paddles certified before October 1 are judged under the standards in force when they were approved, so your existing approved paddle is not affected by the change alone.</p>
          </div>
        </div>

        <div id="check" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            5. How to Check Whether a Paddle Is Approved
          </h2>

          <p>
            The authoritative source is USA Pickleball's own approved paddle list, which it publishes through its equipment site. Search for the brand and the exact model, then confirm the entry shows as a pass. Match the details that distinguish one version from another: thickness (for example 14mm versus 16mm), shape and any generation or model number. A pass for one thickness or generation does not automatically cover another.
          </p>

          <p>
            Do not rely on the seal printed on the paddle alone. USA Pickleball has said that prototype or pre-certification products should not carry the approved mark, and the list can change after a paddle is printed. A paddle can also be removed after certification if later samples fail. The printed seal is a reasonable first signal, but the list is what a tournament director will use, and it is the player's responsibility to confirm their paddle is listed as a pass before match play.
          </p>

          <p>
            Check the list close to the event rather than once when you buy. Listings have changed after paddles were already in players' hands. If your paddle has been removed, you will need a different approved paddle for sanctioned play, even if your copy was legal on the day you bought it. Keeping a backup paddle that is solidly on the list is cheap insurance if you play tournaments regularly.
          </p>

          <p>
            There are also reports of some certifications running for a fixed term with periodic audits, while other sources describe approval as open-ended once granted. Because the reports conflict and the policy could change, the safe approach is the same either way: look the paddle up on the current list before an event instead of assuming a past approval still stands.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="delisting" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            6. Delistings, Investigations and Counterfeits
          </h2>

          <p>
            The list is not static. USA Pickleball has issued public statements removing or suspending specific paddles after testing. It removed the Chorus Fire EX and SX from the approved list after they failed to meet PBCoR standards, and it has published statements about other paddles whose production samples did not match what was certified. Industry outlets have reported further removals and investigations, including paddles found to exceed surface roughness limits and well-known models that faced action over power testing.
          </p>

          <p>
            These cases follow a pattern. A paddle is certified from a submitted sample, retail production then tests differently, and the paddle is removed or placed under review. That can happen through manufacturing variation, design changes between sample and production, or, in the worst cases, deliberate differences. As a buyer you cannot test any of this yourself, which is another reason to check the list rather than trust marketing.
          </p>

          <p>
            Counterfeits are a related problem. Popular paddles are copied and sold through marketplaces at a discount, sometimes with the approved mark printed on them. A counterfeit is not the certified paddle, whatever it says on the face, and it may play very differently. Buy from the manufacturer or an authorized retailer, be wary of prices far below the usual range and keep proof of purchase. Our guide to <a href="/how-much-does-a-pickleball-paddle-cost/">what a pickleball paddle costs</a> gives a sense of normal pricing for each tier.
          </p>

          <p>
            Enforcement at events has also been increasing, with reports of on-site paddle checks at some amateur tournaments. For most players this changes nothing; a genuine paddle that is listed as a pass is fine. It does make it more likely that a delisted or counterfeit paddle will be caught, which is a good outcome for everyone else in the draw.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="recreational" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            7. When Approval Matters, and When It Does Not
          </h2>

          <p>
            If you only play open play at your local courts, in a casual league or with friends, approval is mostly irrelevant. Nobody checks paddles, and any paddle that is safe and comfortable will do. Many recreational players never look at the list. That said, buying an approved paddle costs nothing extra in most cases, keeps your options open if you later enter an event and acts as a basic sanity check on the more extreme marketing claims.
          </p>

          <p>
            If you play sanctioned tournaments, approval matters a great deal. Using a paddle that is not listed as a pass can lead to forfeits if it is identified during play. Check the list before each event, keep the exact model details handy and consider bringing a second approved paddle. Leagues and clubs that run their own events may or may not follow USA Pickleball equipment rules, so ask the organizer.
          </p>

          <p>
            Approval also gives you a useful signal when shopping. A paddle from an established brand that appears on the list has at least been built to known dimensions, surface and power limits. A paddle that is not on the list, and whose seller cannot tell you why, deserves extra scrutiny. Our <a href="/how-to-choose-a-pickleball-paddle/">guide to choosing a paddle</a> covers the rest of the decision, from weight and grip to core thickness.
          </p>

          <p>
            Finally, approval says nothing about how long a paddle will last. Paddles wear, cores soften and faces lose texture with play, and an approved paddle that has been played hard for a year may feel very different from a new one. Our guide to <a href="/how-long-do-pickleball-paddles-last/">how long pickleball paddles last</a> explains what to watch for. For the paddles we actually recommend, approved models included, see our <a href="/best-pickleball-paddle/">best pickleball paddles</a> roundup.
          </p>
        </div>

        <!-- Common Mistakes -->
        <div id="mistakes" class="scroll-mt-24 mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            8. Common Mistakes to Avoid
          </h2>

          <div class="space-y-6 my-8">
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Trusting the printed seal alone</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                The approved mark on a paddle is a starting point, not proof. Paddles have been removed from the list after they were printed, and counterfeits can carry the mark. Look up the exact model on USA Pickleball's current list before any sanctioned event.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Assuming one approval covers every version</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Approval is tied to a specific model. A 14mm version, a new generation or a different shape is a separate paddle that needs its own certification. Match the thickness, shape and model name, not just the brand and product line.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Checking once and never again</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                The list changes as paddles are added, removed or placed under review. If you play tournaments, check your paddle shortly before each event, and keep a backup approved paddle in case your main one is delisted.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Treating approval as a quality rating</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Approval means a paddle met the equipment standards, not that it is good, durable or right for your game. Choose a paddle on how it plays and feels, then confirm it is approved if you need it to be.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Buying a suspiciously cheap name-brand paddle</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Popular approved paddles are counterfeited and sold through marketplaces well below normal prices. A counterfeit is not the certified paddle even if it carries the mark. Buy from the manufacturer or an authorized retailer and keep your receipt.
              </p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

        <!-- FAQ -->
        <div id="faq" class="scroll-mt-24 mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            9. Frequently Asked Questions
          </h2>

          <div class="space-y-6">
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Do I need a USA Pickleball approved paddle?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Only for sanctioned events that follow USA Pickleball rules, where players are responsible for using a paddle listed as a pass on the approved list. For casual and recreational play nobody checks, so any safe, comfortable paddle is fine. Buying an approved paddle still keeps your options open if you later enter a tournament.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">How do I check if my paddle is USA Pickleball approved?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Search USA Pickleball's official approved paddle list, published through its equipment site, for your brand and exact model, and confirm it shows as a pass. Match the thickness, shape and generation, since each is certified separately. Check close to your event, because paddles can be added to or removed from the list.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">What is PBCoR in pickleball?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                PBCoR stands for Paddle/Ball Coefficient of Restitution, a measure of how fast the ball rebounds relative to its impact speed. USA Pickleball introduced it in late 2024 to limit paddle power. Industry reporting puts the limit at 0.44 from November 2024 and 0.43 from November 2025; confirm the current value in USA Pickleball's equipment standards.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">What is the new USA Pickleball spin test?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Announced in July 2026, it measures the actual spin rate of a ball struck in a standardized way using a camera system. According to reporting on the announcement, paddles certified after October 1, 2026 must stay under 2,100 RPM. Paddles certified earlier are held to the standards in effect when they were approved.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Can a paddle lose its approval?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Yes. USA Pickleball has removed paddles from the list after testing, including models that failed the PBCoR power standard, and has published statements about paddles whose production samples did not match the certified sample. If your paddle is removed, it is no longer legal for sanctioned play even if you bought it while it was approved.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Is there a weight or thickness limit for pickleball paddles?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Not in the USA Pickleball amateur rulebook. The size rule limits combined length and width to 24 inches and length to 17 inches, but there is no limit on thickness or weight. Some professional tours have set their own additional limits, so check the rules of any pro or specialty event separately.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Are cheap paddles USA Pickleball approved?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Some are. Approval depends on whether a model was submitted and passed, not on price, and many inexpensive paddles from established brands are on the list. The risk with very cheap paddles is counterfeits and unbranded models that were never submitted. Look the specific paddle up rather than assuming either way.
              </p>
            </div>

          </div>
